13:38:19 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you, and I'm not saying you're making that up or that your experience isn't real. If that's what you saw with your own eyes, that happened. But here's the thing worth sitting with: your high school was one school, with a specific set of guys, in a specific place and time. That's a sample size of maybe a handful of guys out of literally millions of football players who have ever played the position. It's real data, but it's a tiny slice, and it's the slice you happened to be standing next to. A few things could be going on simultaneously without contradicting what you saw: - Maybe your school's athletic program did pull big kids toward football and away from academic tracks early - Maybe the remedial placement had more to do with other factors (learning disabilities, disengagement, home life, teaching quality) that just happened to correlate with kids who were also big - Maybe you're remembering the linemen who fit the pattern more vividly than the ones who didn't, because that's just how memory works None of that means you're wrong about what you observed. It means one local observation, however vivid, isn't strong evidence for a rule about an entire category of people across the world. That's true even when the observation feels rock solid — plenty of confidently-held generalizations turn out to be based on real but narrow experiences. Doesn't mean you have to feel differently about the guys you actually knew. Just worth being cautious about turning "guys I knew" into "how it is."
