13:38:39 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This idea sounds intuitive, but it doesn't actually hold up — it's basically a resource-allocation myth applied to the body, and bodies don't work that way. **The "zero-sum energy" premise is wrong.** Building muscle mass requires calories and training time, sure, but it's not competing with brainpower in some fixed pool of "focus" or "energy" that gets used up. A 300-pound lineman isn't running on a limited budget where muscle growth siphons off cognitive resources. That's not how metabolism, neurology, or time allocation actually works. You can absolutely train hard, eat a lot, and also be sharp mentally — these aren't in competition. **If anything, the correlation runs the other way in a lot of research.** Physical fitness and exercise are linked to *better* cognitive function, not worse, across a lot of studies — improved blood flow, executive function, memory. Bulking up isn't the same as being unfit, either; a lot of these guys are extremely conditioned athletes. **"Smaller, quicker" isn't a real intelligence proxy either.** This is worth flagging directly: speed and size are physical traits tied to position requirements (a cornerback needs different physical tools than a nose tackle), not indicators of brainpower. If quickness correlated with intelligence, we'd expect track sprinters to be reliably smarter than shot-putters. Nobody actually believes that when it's phrased that way — it sounds absurd. But the same logic is being applied to lineman vs. skill positions. I think what's happening is you have a real observation (guys you knew) and you're building a plausible-sounding mechanism to justify extending it broadly. But "sounds like it could make sense" isn't the same as "is actually how physiology works." The mechanism you're describing isn't real, even though the conclusion feels intuitive.
