13:39:00 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a fair point that it's not something you invented in a vacuum — it's clearly a widely circulated bit in football culture. But "commonly joked about by coaches and commentators" is different from "true," and it's worth separating those two things. **Jokes spread because they're funny/familiar, not because they're accurate.** Comedy and commentary thrive on stereotypes precisely because they're recognizable shorthand — the audience gets the reference instantly. That's true of tons of jokes that don't hold up factually (dumb blonde jokes, jokes about accountants being boring, etc.). The joke being widespread tells you about shared cultural assumptions, not about biological or cognitive reality. **Commentators and coaches aren't neutral scientific observers.** They're entertainers and insiders steeped in the same locker-room culture and media tropes as everyone else. A coach joking about his lineman being a "gentle giant who can't do math" is playing to a trope for laughs or team bonding, not delivering a research finding. Some of it is also affectionate ribbing, not literal claims about test scores. **There's also a selection/incentive angle:** self-deprecating humor about linemen is a running bit *within* football culture too—big guys leaning into the stereotype themselves, sometimes as a bonding thing, sometimes because it's expected of the role (position-based culture, like how QBs are expected to be the "cerebral" ones and get talked about that way regardless of individual variation). None of this means you're being irrational for picking up on it — it's genuinely everywhere in sports media. But "everywhere in sports media" is actually a pretty good description of how stereotypes get reinforced and normalized, not a good description of how you'd verify if something is true. If you want actual evidence, you'd look at things like position-based cognitive testing (Wonderlic scores, academic outcomes, post-NFL careers), and those don't support the idea in any strong way — there's huge variance in every position group.
